Use `return` to send a value back to the caller.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    double |value I32| -> I32 {
        return value * 2
    }
]

A function can return more than one success value:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    pair || -> String, I32 {
        return "Priya", 2
    }

    name, count = pair()
]

The number of receiving names must match the number of returned success values.

A call can update an existing binding and create another in the same step.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    total ~= 0
    name_again, ~total = pair()
]

`name_again` receives a new String binding. The `~` on `total` updates the
existing binding to 2.

A function with no success values may fall through. That is not the same as
`-> !`, which means the function never returns at all.

Functions that can fail add one final error return marked with `!`. The Errors
page explains how to propagate or recover from that path.
